    "lawCode": "Amd §579, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A7505\n\nTITLE  OF BILL:   An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto coordinated assessment programs\n\nPURPOSE\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to promote intergovernmental cooperation  by\nclarifying  and simplifying the administration of Coordinated Assessment\nPrograms (CAPs).\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Real Property Tax Law (RPTL)  §  579(1)  to\nclarify  that  any  agreement to establish a CAP must be entered into at\nleast 45 days prior to the relevant taxable status date, and  to  delete\nthe  current  requirement  that  the governing body of the participating\nassessing units must approve such agreement within that same time frame.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends RPTL § 579(3)(a) to clarify that an appoin-\ntive assessor in a CAP is subject to the six-year  term  of  office  set\nforth in RPTL § 310.\n\nSection 3 of the bill makes the following changes to RPTL § 579(4):\n\n* amends paragraph (b) to reduce from 6 months to 45 days the time peri-\nod prior to the relevant taxable status date that a governing body of an\nassessing  unit  must  notify  the State Board of Real Property Services\nthat it intends to withdraw from a CAP;\n\n* amends paragraph (c) to reduce from 6 months to 45 days the time peri-\nod prior to the relevant taxable status date that local laws  or  resol-\nutions providing for the termination of a CAP must be approved;\n\n* adds a new paragraph (d) to provide for the automatic termination of a\nCAP  in the event that the State Board of Real Property Services becomes\naware, on or before the relevant taxable  status  date,  that  the  same\nindividual  is  no longer serving as assessor in all the assessing units\nof a CAP; and\n\n* adds a new paragraph (e) to provide that a CAP shall  continue  unless\nit is terminated pursuant to the provisions of RPTL § 579(4)(c) or (d).\n\nSection 4 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nRPTL  § 579(1) provides that the governing body of two or more assessing\nunits may establish a CAP by entering into an agreement that is approved\nby the governing body of each participating assessing unit at  least  45\ndays before the first assessment role to which such program is to apply.\n\nRPTL  §  579(3)(a)  provides that the same individual shall be appointed\nassessor in each assessing unit participating in a CAP, but the  section\ndoes not specify a term of office for such individual.\n\nRPTL  §  579(4)(b) and (c) provide that a governing body of an assessing\nunit participating in a CAP must notify the State Board of Real Property\nServices at least six months prior to the relevant taxable  status  date\nthat it intends either to withdraw from or terminate a CAP.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis legislative proposal was included in the proposed State Fiscal Year\n2008-2009  budget  (S.6806  &  A.9806, Part T, §§ 14-16), but it was not\nincluded in the adopted budget.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nThe CAP Program was established in 1994 to  provide  for  administrative\nefficiency  in  assessment.  The Program, however, may be underutilized.\nThis bill reduces certain  technical  time  requirements  and  clarifies\nother  procedures that govern the Program in an effort to encourage more\nassessing units to participate. The expansion of the CAP Program  is  an\nimportant step toward the overall goal of consolidating and streamlining\nlocal assessment functions in order to control costs and reduce property\ntaxes.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo  the  extent that this bill leads to greater participation in CAPs by\nassessing units, additional State aid of up $7 per parcel may be payable\nto each additional assessing unit participating in a  CAP,  pursuant  to\nRPTL § 1573.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThe bill takes effect immediately.\n\n",
